Let the I-CSCF re-read the S-CSCF candidate table when a UAA leaves it to
choose.

ims_icscf reads the s_cscf and s_cscf_capabilities tables once, in mod_init,
and closes the database connection on the next statement. From then on the
candidate set is frozen: an S-CSCF that is started, scaled out, or taken away
after the I-CSCF came up is invisible to I_get_capab_ordered() until the
I-CSCF is restarted. In a deployment where the S-CSCF publishes its own row
and the row carries a TTL, that is the whole membership mechanism and it only
ever runs once.

The moment where this matters is the UAA. When the HSS answers a UAR without
naming an S-CSCF, the choice is the I-CSCF's to make out of its own table --
3GPP TS 29.228 6.1.1, the DIAMETER_FIRST_REGISTRATION and
DIAMETER_SERVER_SELECTION cases. That is the one point in the call flow where
the I-CSCF is about to act on the table and can still be told it has changed,
so that is where the re-read goes: at the success label of
async_cdp_uar_callback, guarded by !server_name.len, before the list is built.

The guard is deliberately the absence of a Server-Name and not the presence of
a Server-Capabilities AVP. Verified against Open5GS v2.8.0: its UAA for a first
registration carries Experimental-Result-Code 2001 and neither AVP --

    Experimental-Result
        Vendor-Id = 10415
        Experimental-Result-Code = 2001
    Origin-Host = hss.epc.<realm>
    Auth-Session-State = 1

-- so cxdx_get_capabilities() returns 0 with every count left at zero, and
I_get_capab_ordered() then matches every cached row with score 0. The table
decides the selection on its own. A trigger conditioned on the AVP would never
fire there, while the staleness it is meant to fix is exactly what is deciding
the outcome.

The cache had to move into shared memory for this. SCSCF_Capabilities was a
process global holding a shm pointer, written once before the fork, so every
process inherited the same value and nothing ever wrote it again. A re-read
from a worker breaks both halves of that: cdp forks its workers
(diameter_peer.c diameter_peer_start(), fork_process("cdp_worker")), so the
new array would be visible only to whichever worker happened to take the
answer, and the shm_free() of the old one would leave every other process --
and the SIP workers running I_scscf_select -- reading freed memory. The set
now lives in a shm scscf_capabilities_cache behind a lock that
I_get_capab_ordered() holds for its whole walk. Readers copy the names out
into their own entries, so the critical section stays short and the returned
list is independent of the cache.

The new set is built into a private array and only swapped in once it is
complete, so the database round trip happens with no lock held and the
previous set keeps serving selections while it runs. The old array is freed
after the lock is released, which is safe precisely because readers hold it: a
reader that started before the swap has finished by then, and one that starts
after it reads the new pointer.

A read that fails or comes back empty leaves the cache alone. An S-CSCF that
is up must not become unroutable because the store was briefly unreachable,
and that is the difference between a table read once at startup, where an
empty result is a configuration error worth shouting about, and one read
repeatedly, where it is a transient.

scscf_reload_interval bounds the cost. It is claimed before the database is
touched rather than after, so a burst of answers spread over the cdp workers
costs one read of the table and not one per answer; a failed read does not
give the slot back, so an unreachable store is retried on the next interval
rather than on the next registration. The default is 0 -- never re-read, which
is what the module did before -- so nothing changes for an existing
configuration that does not set it.

Two leaks in db.c come along, because they are one-shot today and recurring
once this path runs more than once. ims_icscf_db_get_scscf() abandons a
partially built set on its error path while telling the caller 0, and
ims_icscf_db_get_capabilities() returns early without freeing the result when
the capabilities table is empty -- which is the normal case for a deployment
that does not use capabilities at all. The free in I_get_capabilities() also
never released scscf_name.s, only the capability arrays.

One consequence worth stating: with the re-read enabled, an empty table at
startup is no longer permanent. mod_init still logs it and carries on, and the
first UAA that needs the table picks up whatever is in it by then. The
ordering constraint that made the I-CSCF wait for an S-CSCF to publish its row
before starting is relaxed, not the requirement itself.

The LIA path has the same shape -- cxdx_lir.c selects from the table when the
answer carries no Server-Name, for a terminating request to an unregistered
user -- and is deliberately left alone here.

